Title: The Ninth Floor
Credit: Written by
Author: Test corpus, public domain
Draft date: 2026-08-24

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - LOBBY - MORNING

Mild morning light on quiet stone. NOOR HADDAD, 30s, a safety inspector, signs the visitor book slowly and tidily. The guard, TOMAS REYES, 50s, nods her through without hurry.

TOMAS
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

TOMAS
Hello.

She thanks him gently and waits for the slow elevator.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - CORRIDOR - MORNING

A plain new corridor. Glass offices, ordinary doors, a mild smell of paint. Noor walks it slowly, tapping the wall panels with the back of her pen.

NOOR
Hello.

A young facilities tech, GABRIEL OYELOWO, 20s, catches up at an easy walk.

GABRIEL
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - EAST OFFICES - DAY

Even light. Plants on the sills. A quiet hum of ordinary work. Noor checks the sprinkler heads one at a time and marks a clean column down her clipboard.

GABRIEL
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

GABRIEL
Hello.

Noor writes the remark down, meaning it, and moves to the next head.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - MECHANICAL ROOM - DAY

Cool and orderly. Pipe runs labelled in fresh tape. PRIYA VANCE, 40s, project engineer, looks up from her screen. A locked ledger sits closed at her elbow, untouched all morning.

PRIYA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

PRIYA
Hello.

They shake hands like two people who expect an ordinary week.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - BREAK ROOM - DAY

A plain room. Coffee going. A tenant, MARIA CASTELLANOS, 60s, sorts a stack of permit files at the table with unhurried patience.

MARIA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

MARIA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

Noor sets her binder beside Maria's files and drinks the coffee slowly.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - STAIRWELL B - DAY

Plain, well marked, quiet. Noor pushes the fire door, watches its swing, and lets it close under its own weight. It seals cleanly.

NOOR
Hello.

She photographs the closer and starts down to read the landing signage. Every sign says exactly what it should say.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - LOBBY - EVENING

Even evening light. Tomas is packing up his post. Noor drops her visitor pass in the tray, tired in a mild and ordinary way.

TOMAS
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

TOMAS
Hello.

She walks out into a plain, unremarkable street.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - CORRIDOR - MORNING

The same corridor, one day later. A ceiling tile has been shoved aside and left that way. Noor stops under it, uneasy, and checks her notes twice. Something is wrong here and she cannot yet name the trouble.

NOOR
Hello.

Gabriel arrives at a hurried walk, worried, wiping his hands on a rag. He avoids the tile and avoids her question.

GABRIEL
Hello.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - MECHANICAL ROOM - DAY

The fresh tape is peeled back on one run. Priya is on the phone, arguing, and ends the call abruptly when Noor enters. The refusal in the room is obvious and unpleasant.

PRIYA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

PRIYA
Hello.

The room is a good deal colder and more hostile than it was yesterday, and both of them know it.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - RECORDS OFFICE - DAY

A harsh fluorescent hum. Noor pulls the permit binder and finds two pages missing, the staple forced open and pressed flat again. Somebody has interfered with the record and hidden the damage.

NOOR
Hello.

MARIA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

MARIA
Hello.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - EAST OFFICES - DAY

The plants are gone and half the desks are stripped. Noor re-checks the sprinkler heads she signed off and finds four of them capped, painted over and disabled. The deception is careless and deliberate.

NOOR
Hello.

GABRIEL
Hello.

She looks at him for a long, unfriendly while. He studies the carpet, ashamed.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - STAIRWELL B - DAY

The good door is propped open with a fire extinguisher. Beyond it the stairwell is stacked with pallets of insulation, narrowing the landing to half its width.

NOOR
Hello.

She lifts the extinguisher clear, unhappy. The door will not seal now. Something in the frame has been cut away and the damage was hidden on purpose.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - MECHANICAL ROOM - NIGHT

Noor works alone with a flashlight, tense, photographing every finding she can reach. Priya appears in the doorway, tired, holding two coffees she will not drink.

PRIYA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

PRIYA
Hello.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - LOBBY - NIGHT

Cold rain on black glass. Tomas is not at his post. An unfriendly stranger in a contractor jacket is, and refuses to look up when Noor badges out. Her badge reads red. She tries again. Red. She is being shut out deliberately and the hostility is unmistakable.

NOOR
Hello.

The stranger says nothing at all and turns his back, which is its own unpleasant and threatening answer.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Smoke! Thin, sweet, poisonous smoke slides along the ceiling in a flat grey sheet, and the alarm is dead and silent. Noor runs the corridor tearing doors open, screaming.

NOOR
Hello.

Nothing answers her but the smoke, thickening horribly, faster than any smoke has a right to move.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - NINTH FLOOR - EAST OFFICES - NIGHT

Fire crawls the ceiling where the murdered sprinkler heads should have drowned it. Maria is trapped behind a jammed glass door, pounding, terrified, choking.

MARIA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

Noor swings the extinguisher and the door bursts in a violent white spray of broken fragments.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - STAIRWELL B - NIGHT

The blocked landing is a roaring wall of burning insulation. Vicious heat slams them backward. Gabriel is already there, bleeding badly from the scalp, panicked and screaming.

GABRIEL
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

Behind them the corridor erupts in a savage orange flash.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - MECHANICAL ROOM - NIGHT

Flames devour the peeled tape run and the room shrieks with murderous pressure. Priya tears the shaft hatch open with a pry bar, screaming over the roar, terrified, and shoves the bleeding Maria through first.

PRIYA
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

The ceiling collapses behind them in a violent torrent of sparks, molten debris and horrible, deafening noise. The heat is savage enough to kill.

INT. MERIDIAN TOWER - SERVICE SHAFT - NIGHT

Black, roasting, deafening. They fight down rung by rung with the fire chasing them, devouring the shaft wall. Gabriel slips and screams in terror. Noor catches his wrist and holds his whole weight on one agonised, tearing arm.

GABRIEL
Hello.

NOOR
Hello.

He climbs, sobbing. Below them the eighth floor door glows orange, buckles, and begins to fail. If it fails they are dead.

EXT. MERIDIAN TOWER - LOADING DOCK - NIGHT

They fall out into the rain as the ninth floor detonates above them, blasting its windows out in one roaring exhale of flame, glass and burning debris. Screaming. Sirens. Terror. Blood on the wet concrete, and a horrible rain of molten wreckage smashing down around them.

NOOR
Hello.

PRIYA
Hello.

The tower burns and howls above them, savage, murderous and unstoppable. Nothing on this street can fight it.

INT. HEARING ROOM - DAY

Quiet. Plain morning light through high windows. Noor sets the photographs on the table one at a time, unhurried, and reads from her notes in an even voice.

NOOR
Hello.

PRIYA
Hello.

Noor slides the last photograph across the table, and the room simply listens.
